IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `cbca-core`

-- Canada Business Corporations Act (R.S.C. 1985, c. C-44), Parts XI to XIV.1 (piece cb3) -- NOUNS.
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. The text this was encoded from is a plain-text rendering of the Department of Justice
-- Canada consolidation current to 2026-09-30 (last amended 2026-03-26); it is not an official version,
-- and nothing here has been verified against the official text. Contains information licensed under
-- the Open Government Licence - Canada.
--
-- WHAT THIS MODULE IS. The types that two or more cb3 modules need, and nothing else.
-- (1) The ceiling of a penalty on summary conviction. Every offence in Parts XI to XIV states a ceiling,
-- not an amount: "liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding five thousand dollars or to
-- imprisonment for a term not exceeding six months or to both". The sentence actually passed is a fact
-- about a case and is never computed here.
-- (2) The five purposes for which a record date may be fixed (s 134(1)); used by cb3-part12-meetings.l4
-- (s 134(2)).
-- It holds no rule of the Act. The rest of the shared vocabulary is in cbca-core.l4 (the keel).

§ `Nouns of Parts XI to XIV.1`

§§ `The ceiling of a penalty on summary conviction`

-- The two ceilings an offence section states. A section that states no imprisonment (s 149(3),
-- s 159(2), s 160(3)) has NOTHING there: the text is silent about imprisonment, which is not the same
-- as a ceiling of nought months.
-- src:1581-1582 is the first of them (s 130(4)); the others are quoted where they are used.
-- src:1581 | Offence
-- src:1582 | (4) An insider who contravenes subsection (1) or (2) is guilty of an offence and liable on summary conviction to a fine not exceeding the greater of one million dollars and three times the profit made, or to imprisonment for a term not exceeding six months or to both.
@ref CBCA s 130(4) offence, the shape of every ceiling in Parts XI to XIV (src:1581-1582)
DECLARE `Penalty ceiling in Parts XI to XIV` HAS
    `maximum fine in dollars` IS A NUMBER
    `maximum imprisonment in months` IS A MAYBE NUMBER

§§ `The purposes of a record date -- s 134(1)`

-- The five purposes for which the directors may fix a record date, as the Act lists them. s 134(2) (no
-- record date fixed) gives a different default for notice, for voting and for every other purpose.
-- src:1646 | 134 (1) The directors may, within the prescribed period, fix in advance a date as the record date for the purpose of determining shareholders
-- src:1647 | (a) entitled to receive payment of a dividend;
-- src:1648 | (b) entitled to participate in a liquidation distribution;
-- src:1649 | (c) entitled to receive notice of a meeting of shareholders;
-- src:1650 | (d) entitled to vote at a meeting of shareholders; or
-- src:1651 | (e) for any other purpose.
@ref CBCA s 134(1) record date purposes (src:1646-1651)
DECLARE `Purpose of a record date under s 134(1)` IS ONE OF
    `a record date for receiving payment of a dividend`
    `a record date for participating in a liquidation distribution`
    `a record date for receiving notice of a meeting of shareholders`
    `a record date for voting at a meeting of shareholders`
    `a record date for any other purpose`
